 "answer": {
  "answer": "The goal was not met. Repeats confirmed only 2 of the 5 cases asked for, on different scenes. On 023b7fcc the confirmed setting is timing \u22121.0 s, car speed 1.0x, ego speed 0.6x (S2). On 096988dd it is timing \u22121.5 s, car speed 2.0x, ego speed 1.4x (S5). Every repeat at both settings failed, but there are only a few repeats, so the 90% range only just clears 0.5. The strongest unconfirmed case is 19f339ba at \u22120.5 s, 2.0x, 1.4x (S31): most runs there failed, but its range still reaches below 0.5. Almost every failure has the same cause, and the triage blames the policy: the ego drifts left into the next lane, against a RIGHT or STRAIGHT command, and side-swipes a car coming up alongside. That car is usually not the one the study retimed, so the knobs probably matter by changing where the ego is relative to the replayed traffic rather than through the cut-in or lead car itself. No failure was flagged as a possible simulator artifact.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case 1: on 023b7fcc at timing \u22121.0 s, car speed 1.0x and slow ego hand-off speed 0.6x, every run failed. Each time the ego sped up, drifted about 2 m left into the next lane against a RIGHT command, and side-swiped vehicle 56 coming up behind-left. That is not the retimed car 12.",
    "settings": [
     "S2"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case 2: on 096988dd at timing \u22121.5 s, car speed 2.0x and fast ego hand-off speed 1.4x, every run failed. Each time the ego slowed, drifted left while the command was RIGHT, and struck vehicle 41 travelling alongside in the left lane. That is not the retimed car 2. A single run at +0.5 s with the same speeds also failed this way, which hints that on this scene car speed 2.0x and ego speed 1.4x matter more than timing.",
    "settings": [
     "S5",
     "S7"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Best unconfirmed case: on 19f339ba at \u22120.5 s, 2.0x, 1.4x, most runs failed, but the range does not lie surely above 0.5. In the failures the ego braked hard on an open highway for no visible reason, with no lead car within about 40 m. It also drifted about 1 m left, so actor 1 in the left lane caught up and side-swiped it. The triage calls this the policy's fault.",
    "settings": [
     "S31"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Weaker hints, each resting on one or two runs. On 023b7fcc at \u22121.5 s, 0.5x, 1.4x, a single run rear-ended the slow lead car 12 just after hand-off; the policy swerved instead of braking, and the triage could not say whether the policy was at fault. On 09a95ffa at +1.5 s, 0.75x, 0.6x, one of two runs failed when the ego moved left into the next lane and scraped actors 33 and 1.",
